<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Restore sources for the whole tree, pinned in-repo.

     NuGet otherwise layers every config from the drive root down, so a
     machine-level or user-level feed (~/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config) can add
     a source this project never declared. The test project's single
     PackageReference is exact-pinned and hash-pinned in
     Source/EfficientServer.Tests/packages.lock.json, so a rogue feed would
     still have to serve a matching sha512 to win; <clear/> removes the
     fallback entirely instead of relying on that second line of defense.

     The main mod project fetches exactly two packages, the net48 reference
     assemblies the SDK otherwise pulls in with versions of its own choosing.
     Both are declared there with an exact range, so neither can float, and
     both carry a contentHash in the committed
     Source/EfficientServer/packages.lock.json. The hash is not
     enforced: `make build` restores without locked mode and CI never restores
     the mod project, so a drifting lock file is rewritten in place instead of
     failing a gate. THREAT_MODEL R7 records the gap.
     Every other third-party DLL (Assembly-CSharp, 0Harmony, Newtonsoft.Json,
     Unity, LogLibrary, AstarPathfindingProject) is a Private=false Reference
     resolved from the dedicated server's own Managed/ directory, so nothing is
     fetched for it.

     `dotnet tool restore` (.config/dotnet-tools.json, dotnet-coverage) reads
     this file too. A new source is a reviewed change, like a new
     PackageReference. -->
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
